  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "632532e6",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Basic Bagging classifier\n",
    "\n",
    "The following code creates a bagged ensemble of regression trees."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 2,
   "id": "d1e9850c",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from sklearn.ensemble import BaggingClassifier\n",
    "from sklearn.tree import DecisionTreeClassifier\n",
    "\n",
    "bag = BaggingClassifier(\n",
    "    estimator=DecisionTreeClassifier(random_state=0),\n",
    "    n_estimators=300,\n",
    "    max_samples=1.0,\n",
    "    bootstrap=True,\n",
    "    oob_score=True,\n",
    "    random_state=0,\n",
    ")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e426af8d",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The important arguments are:\n",
    "\n",
    "- `estimator`: the base model. For classical tree bagging, this is a decision tree.\n",
    "- `n_estimators`: the number of bootstrap models.\n",
    "- `max_samples`: the sample size used for each bootstrap sample.\n",
    "- `bootstrap`: whether sampling is done with replacement. `True` gives bagging; `False` gives pasting.\n",
    "- `oob_score`: whether to compute out-of-bag performance.\n",
    "\n",
    "After creating the model, we use the same `.fit()` and `.predict()` and `.score()` workflow as before. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 3,
   "id": "925bf685",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "0.9138888888888889"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 3,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "bag.fit(X_train, y_train)\n",
    "bag.score(X_test, y_test)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e048713c",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Random Forest Classifier\n",
    "\n",
    "A random forest classifier is similar, but the random feature selection is built in."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 4,
   "id": "637fa92a",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "0.9138888888888889"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 4,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "from sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestClassifier\n",
    "\n",
    "rf = RandomForestClassifier(\n",
    "    n_estimators=300,\n",
    "    max_features=1.0,\n",
    "    bootstrap=True,\n",
    "    oob_score=True,\n",
    "    random_state=0\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "rf.fit(X_train, y_train)\n",
    "rf.score(X_test, y_test)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "7845d6d0",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The most important additional argument is `max_features`.\n",
    "\n",
    "For example:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 5,
   "id": "cd59a432",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "#| eval: false\n",
    "RandomForestClassifier(max_features=1.0)\n",
    "RandomForestClassifier(max_features=\"sqrt\")\n",
    "RandomForestClassifier(max_features=0.5)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "79b8fea7",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "- `1.0`: consider all predictors at each split. In this case, it is reduced to a `BaggingRegressor`.\n",
    "- `\"sqrt\"`: consider about $\\sqrt p$ predictors at each split.\n",
    "- `0.5`: consider half of the predictors at each split.\n",
    "\n",
    "When `max_features=1.0`, a random forest regressor is very close to bagging with decision trees. When `max_features` is smaller, the trees become more decorrelated. \n",
    "\n",
    "### Useful Attributes\n",
    "\n",
    "After fitting a bagging or random forest model, several attributes are useful. This example shows random forest, but bagging also supports these attributes.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "- `.estimators_` stores the fitted trees, and we could indicate which tree we want to use by calling the index."
   ]
